+   - **Objection:** a reply is written, so this is the one attempt [BK09b-K3]: acknowledge it in their words, answer once with one proof item or a clarifying question, offer a smaller step. Never argue, never repeat the pitch. If they push back again or repeat the no, close graciously; that no is final. Sales-book scripts we don't use [BK09a-K6]: "send me info" means ask which part and send that one thing; "not my decision" means ask once who owns it, never hint at going over their head; "call me later" is not now. Never press for the meeting after a no or assume a hidden objection.
+   - **Not now:** thank them, confirm the timing they gave, and ask permission to check back then ("Shall I drop you a line in January?"). If they already named a date ("try me in January"), that is permission for one check-in at that date. Without permission, no further contact, and never a default re-contact date (for example 60 days) [BK09b-K4; policy].
    - **Not interested:** a one-line, gracious close is optional ("Understood, thanks for letting me know. I won't follow up."). Recommend no further outreach from this sender. If the wording leans towards "stop contacting me", label it unsubscribe instead.
    - **Wrong person / referral:** thank them. If they named someone, ask nothing more of them. Log the named person as a new lead that must pass the prospecting compliance checks (jurisdiction, legal form, source) before any contact. A first email to the referred person may mention the referrer only if the referrer actually suggested the contact; it is a new first touch with a full footer.
    - **Unsubscribe:** default to no reply. If the person asked for confirmation, draft one line: "Done, you won't hear from me again." No selling, no "sorry to see you go" pitch.
    - **Angry / complaint:** default to a short, sincere reply the owner can choose to send: a brief apology, confirmation they are removed, and, if they asked, where their address came from. No justification, no debate, no legal argument. Flag to the owner; repeated complaints should trigger a campaign review.
+   - **Missed meeting** (an apology, or the owner reports a no-show; label Other): one miss gets one offer to rebook with a specific time. After two, draft one clarity email for the owner (still a priority, bad timing or not a fit?) and recommend closing as lost if it goes unanswered [BK09b-K4].
 
